===============================================================================
Testing ENETC4 VF with DPDK using vfio-pci (No-IOMMU mode) on i.MX95 EVK
===============================================================================

**************************************************************************
WARNING: this procedure DISABLES the ARM SMMU-v3 for the ENTIRE SoC.
         Every DMA-capable device then runs WITHOUT IOMMU isolation or
         protection (no DMA containment). This is acceptable for DPDK
         bring-up and evaluation only. Do NOT use this configuration on
         a production or security-sensitive image without fully
         understanding the SoC-wide loss of DMA isolation.
**************************************************************************

Overview
========
This guide describes how to run an ENETC4 Virtual Function (VF) with DPDK on
the i.MX95 EVK using the vfio-pci kernel driver in VFIO No-IOMMU mode
(IOVA = physical address).

By default the ENETC4 VF is used with uio_pci_generic or igb_uio. This guide
covers the additional setup that is required to use vfio-pci instead.


Background: why a special setup is needed
=========================================
Two independent hardware/software constraints on i.MX95 must be satisfied at
the same time:

1. The i.MX95 ARM SMMU-v3 is NON-coherent (IDR0.COHACC = 0). When a device is
   behind the SMMU it gets an IOMMU group, and the VFIO core rejects binding it
   with an IOMMU because the device does not advertise
   IOMMU_CAP_CACHE_COHERENCY. The vfio-pci bind then fails with -EINVAL (-22).

2. The ENETC VSI-to-PSI mailbox (used by the VF to talk to the PF for MAC
   programming, link status, promiscuous mode, etc.) is an internal ENETC
   station-interface DMA copy. That DMA copies the VF send buffer
   (VSIMSGSNDAR) into the PF receive buffer (PSIVMSGRCVAR). If the SMMU is
   active but the ENETC SI has no valid stream-ID mapping, this internal copy
   is blocked and every message times out ("VSI to PSI message timeout" in the
   kernel, "Message not processed by PSI" in the DPDK PMD).

The only configuration that satisfies BOTH at once is to disable the SMMU
completely, so the whole SoC runs with direct (untranslated, physical) DMA.
This is the "No-IOMMU everywhere" model, and it matches the VFIO No-IOMMU
bootargs used below.


Prerequisites
=============
1. Kernel must be built with VFIO and VFIO No-IOMMU support:
        CONFIG_VFIO=y
        CONFIG_VFIO_PCI=y
        CONFIG_VFIO_NOIOMMU=y
   Verify on the running target:
        zcat /proc/config.gz | grep -i -e VFIO -e NOIOMMU
   (or check the kernel .config used to build the image).

2. DPDK built for arm64. Refer to the 'Cross compiled toolchain For ARM64'
   section in nxp/README:
        $ meson arm64-build --cross-file config/arm/arm64_imx_linux_gnu_gcc -Dexamples=all
        $ ninja -C arm64-build

3. Hugepages available on the target (configured via bootargs below or at
   runtime with: echo 1024 > /proc/sys/vm/nr_hugepages).


Required Device Tree change
===========================
File: arch/arm64/boot/dts/freescale/imx95.dtsi

Two edits are needed in this file. The exact line numbers vary between kernel
releases / BSP tags, so locate the nodes by name rather than by line number.

Change 1 - disable the SMMU node
--------------------------------
Find the "smmu: iommu@490d0000" node and add a disabled status to it:

        smmu: iommu@490d0000 {
                ...
                interrupt-names = "eventq", "gerror", "priq", "cmdq-sync";
                #iommu-cells = <1>;
                status = "disabled";          /* <-- add this line */
        };

Change 2 - remove the ENETC iommu-map
-------------------------------------
Find the "netc_bus0: pcie@4ca00000" node and delete the entire "iommu-map"
property (the block that maps the ENETC functions to the SMMU). Replacing it
with an explanatory comment is recommended:

        netc_bus0: pcie@4ca00000 {
                ...
                <0x90 &its 0x65 0x1>, //ENETC2 VF0
                <0xa0 &its 0x66 0x1>, //ENETC2 VF1
                <0xc0 &its 0x67 0x1>; //NETC Timer

                /* Delete the whole iommu-map property, i.e. these lines:
                 *      iommu-map = <0x0  &smmu 0x20 0x1>,
                 *                  <0x10 &smmu 0x21 0x1>,
                 *                  <0x20 &smmu 0x22 0x1>,
                 *                  <0x40 &smmu 0x23 0x1>,
                 *                  <0x80 &smmu 0x24 0x1>,
                 *                  <0x90 &smmu 0x25 0x1>,
                 *                  <0xa0 &smmu 0x26 0x1>,
                 *                  <0xc0 &smmu 0x27 0x1>;
                 *
                 * iommu-map intentionally omitted. The i.MX95 SMMU-v3 is
                 * non-coherent (IDR0.COHACC=0), so VFIO with an IOMMU rejects
                 * these ENETC functions (vfio needs IOMMU_CAP_CACHE_COHERENCY).
                 * Leaving the ENETC endpoints out of the SMMU lets DPDK bind
                 * them via vfio-pci no-iommu mode (enable_unsafe_noiommu_mode=1).
                 * The kernel fsl_enetc/fsl_enetc_vf drivers still work because
                 * they use the streaming DMA API.
                 */
        };

Rebuild the DTB and deploy it, then reboot the board with the new DTB.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE: why these DTS changes are required
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Disabling the SMMU node stops the ARM SMMU-v3 driver from probing, so NO
  device on the SoC gets an IOMMU group. This is what allows vfio-pci to bind
  the ENETC VF in No-IOMMU mode (without an IOMMU group the VFIO core does not
  apply the cache-coherency check that otherwise fails on i.MX95 and returns
  -EINVAL).
- With the SMMU disabled, all DMA is untranslated (direct physical). The ENETC
  SI VSI-to-PSI mailbox DMA can therefore reach the PF receive buffer, so the
  VF-to-PF messaging works (no more "VSI to PSI message timeout"). Both the
  kernel fsl_enetc_vf driver and the DPDK VF PMD rely on this mailbox.
- IMPORTANT: this disables the SMMU for the ENTIRE SoC, not just ENETC. Every
  DMA-capable device now runs without IOMMU isolation/protection. This is
  acceptable for DPDK bring-up/evaluation but should not be shipped on a
  production/secure image without understanding the loss of DMA isolation.
- Consequence of the above: 'iommu.passthrough=1' becomes redundant (there is
  no active IOMMU to pass through), and the removed 'iommu-map' entries on the
  ENETC PCIe host bridge are moot while the SMMU is disabled.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------


Required bootargs (mmcargs)
===========================
Add the following to the kernel command line:

        vfio.enable_unsafe_noiommu_mode=1 vfio_platform.reset_required=0 iommu.passthrough=1 default_hugepagesz=2m hugepagesz=2m hugepages=1024

Note on the individual parameters:
- vfio.enable_unsafe_noiommu_mode=1 : enable VFIO No-IOMMU mode (required).
- vfio_platform.reset_required=0    : do not require a platform reset handler.
- iommu.passthrough=1               : redundant when the SMMU is disabled (see
                                      the NOTE above); kept only for clarity and
                                      harmless to leave in.
- default_hugepagesz / hugepagesz / hugepages : reserve 2MB hugepages for DPDK.

Example (u-boot):

        setenv mmcargs <user-specific-params> isolcpus=1 vfio.enable_unsafe_noiommu_mode=1 vfio_platform.reset_required=0 iommu.passthrough=1 default_hugepagesz=2m hugepagesz=2m hugepages=1024

After boot, verify:

        cat /proc/cmdline


Runtime steps before running dpdk-l2fwd
=======================================
Run all steps below on the A-core Linux console.

In the steps below <pf-pci-addr> is the PF PCI address and <vf-pci-addr> is
the VF PCI address. These are board/slot specific and differ between i.MX95 EVK
configurations, so always read the actual values from dpdk-devbind.py -s and
dmesg (illustrative values 0002:00:10.0 for the PF and 0002:00:12.0 for the VF
are shown only as examples).

1. (Optional) Enable VFIO No-IOMMU mode at runtime if it was not set via
   bootargs:
        echo 1 > /sys/module/vfio/parameters/enable_unsafe_noiommu_mode

2. Make sure the PF is bound to the kernel driver (nxp_enetc4 / fsl_enetc4) and
   its link is up. Use dpdk-devbind.py -s to identify the PF PCI address
   <pf-pci-addr> (for example 0002:00:10.0 for the 10G port) and its eth
   interface:
        dpdk-devbind.py -s

3. Create one VF from the PF:
        echo 1 > /sys/bus/pci/devices/<pf-pci-addr>/sriov_numvfs

   Check dmesg to identify the VF PCI address <vf-pci-addr>, for example:
        fsl_enetc_vf 0002:00:12.0: enabling device (0000 -> 0002)
   As per the above log, 0002:00:12.0 is the <vf-pci-addr>.

4. (Optional) Enable trusted mode for the VF from the PF (required for some
   features such as setting the primary MAC and promiscuous mode). eth1 here is
   the PF eth interface for the 10G port:
        ip link set eth1 vf 0 trust on

5. Bind the VF to vfio-pci:
        echo vfio-pci > /sys/bus/pci/devices/<vf-pci-addr>/driver_override
        echo <vf-pci-addr> > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/fsl_enetc_vf/unbind
        echo <vf-pci-addr> > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/vfio-pci/bind

6. Verify the bind and No-IOMMU group:
        dpdk-devbind.py -s
   Expect the VF to show: drv=vfio-pci
   dmesg should show:
        vfio-pci 0002:00:12.0: Adding kernel taint for vfio-noiommu group on device
        vfio-pci 0002:00:12.0: vfio-noiommu device opened by user (...)


Run the application
===================
        $ ./dpdk-l2fwd -c 0x3 -a <vf-pci-addr> -- -p 0x1 -T 0

Note on the -c 0x3 core mask: it selects cores 0 and 1. Core 0 is used as the
EAL main lcore and core 1 (isolated via isolcpus=1 in the bootargs above) runs
the packet forwarding thread. Isolating the forwarding core keeps the kernel
scheduler off it for stable poll-mode performance while the main lcore still
runs on the non-isolated core 0.

Other examples:
        $ ./dpdk-testpmd -c 0x3 -a <vf-pci-addr> -- -i --portmask=0x1
        $ ./dpdk-l3fwd -c 0x2 -a <vf-pci-addr> -- -p 0x1 -P --config="(0,0,1)"


Expected good output
====================
        EAL: Selected IOVA mode 'PA'
        EAL: VFIO support initialized
        EAL: Using IOMMU type 8 (No-IOMMU)
   - No "Message not processed by PSI" / "VSI message send error" messages.
   - Port link comes up and traffic flows.


Troubleshooting
===============
- "VSI to PSI message timeout" (kernel) or "Message not processed by PSI"
  (DPDK):
    * The SMMU is still active. Confirm the DTS change was applied AND the
      updated DTB was actually deployed/booted (cat /proc/cmdline shows the
      right image; check that the SMMU driver did not probe).
    * The PF is not bound to nxp_enetc4/fsl_enetc4 or its link is down. The PF
      services the mailbox, so it must remain in the kernel and be link-up.

- vfio-pci bind fails with "write error: Invalid argument" / dmesg
  "probe with driver vfio-pci failed with error -22":
    * VFIO No-IOMMU mode is not enabled (check
      /sys/module/vfio/parameters/enable_unsafe_noiommu_mode and the bootarg).
    * The SMMU is still enabled, so the VF still has an IOMMU group and the
      cache-coherency check fails. Disable the SMMU as described above.

- Tunable mailbox timeout/delay (optional), useful when debugging:
        export ENETC4_VSI_WAIT_DELAY_UPDATE=<val>
        export ENETC4_VSI_WAIT_TIMEOUT_UPDATE=<val>

  These override the built-in defaults used while the VF polls the PSI for a
  mailbox response:
    * ENETC4_VSI_WAIT_DELAY_UPDATE   : per-iteration delay in MICROSECONDS
      (passed to rte_delay_us). Default 2000 (i.e. 2 ms). Practical range is
      a few hundred us to a few ms; too small busy-polls harder, too large
      slows down message completion.
    * ENETC4_VSI_WAIT_TIMEOUT_UPDATE : number of poll ITERATIONS (a plain
      count, not a time unit) before the VF gives up. Default 100. The total
      wait is roughly DELAY x TIMEOUT (default 2000 us x 100 = ~200 ms).
      Increase this if a slow/loaded PF needs longer to service the mailbox.


Notes
=====
- The PF must remain bound to the kernel driver (nxp_enetc4 / fsl_enetc4) and
  be link-up; it is the PSI side that services the VF (VSI) mailbox messages.
- Link-status-change (LSC) interrupts on the VF are supported with the igb_uio
  framework. With vfio-pci No-IOMMU the l2fwd example above uses -T 0 (polling).
- Disabling the SMMU removes DMA isolation SoC-wide. Use this configuration for
  DPDK bring-up/evaluation only.
